---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const relationshipMappings = [
  ["First ever", "first", "ever", "Earliest matching activity for the entity"],
  ["Last ever", "last", "ever", "Latest matching activity for the entity"],
  ["Last before", "last", "before", "Latest matching activity before the cohort row"],
  ["First after", "first", "after", "Earliest matching activity after the cohort row"],
  ["First in between", "first", "in_between", "Earliest match before the cohort repeats"],
  ["Aggregate before", "metric", "before", "Aggregate matching activity rows before the cohort"],
];

const changes = [
  {
    number: "01",
    title: "Fetch and relation become separate choices",
    body: "V2 gave useful combinations names such as last before. V3 keeps those readable phrases while exposing the two decisions underneath: which matching row or aggregate to fetch, and where in time to look for it.",
  },
  {
    number: "02",
    title: "Time boundaries become explicit",
    body: "Before and after remain strict by default. Before or on and after or on make equal-timestamp behavior intentional. In-between uses the current cohort timestamp and the next cohort timestamp as a defined interval.",
  },
  {
    number: "03",
    title: "Temporal relations and object joins work together",
    body: "A temporal relation answers when an activity happened. A join answers whether two rows refer to the same order, product, account, subscription, or other business object. V3 represents both instead of asking one concept to do two jobs.",
  },
  {
    number: "04",
    title: "Dimensions include time-valid history",
    body: "A query may attach stable reference data with direct keys or choose the dimension version that was valid when an activity happened. This brings slowly changing dimensions (SCD) into the query contract without copying changing attributes into every activity.",
  },
  {
    number: "05",
    title: "Every query declares its row grain",
    body: "The cohort still owns the initial rows. First, last, and metric appends reduce matches before returning columns to that grain. An all append is different: it intentionally returns every match and can expand the result. V3 says that plainly.",
  },
  {
    number: "06",
    title: "Filters become typed, bounded input",
    body: "Each cohort or append may carry a small list of typed filters. The initial grammar combines them with AND and limits them to direct stream columns, so a compiler can validate values and operators without accepting executable SQL fragments.",
  },
  {
    number: "07",
    title: "The query plan becomes portable input",
    body: "A structured plan can be validated, explained, stored, reviewed, and compiled for a warehouse dialect. SQL becomes an output of the semantic contract instead of the only place the contract exists.",
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Activity Schema V3 proposal — Activity Schema"
  description="A proposal for Activity Schema V3: separate fetch from temporal relation, add typed filters, joins, and slowly changing dimensions, define row grain, and make query compilation portable."
  path="/v3/"
>
  <header class="page-hero shell article-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Proposal · August 2026</p>
    <h1>Activity Schema V3 makes querying a first-class contract.</h1>
    <p class="lede">V2 standardized the activity stream and introduced temporal relationships. This V3 proposal formalizes what we learned by using that model in production: <strong>fetch, relation, filters, joins, dimensions, row grain, and compilation should be explicit.</strong></p>
  </header>

  <aside class="shell status-note" aria-label="Proposal status">
    <span class="mono">STATUS</span>
    <p><strong>This is a proposal, not yet the canonical specification.</strong> The canonical repository currently publishes Activity Schema 2.0. This article is being published on the project site while access to that repository is restored, so the ideas can be reviewed and improved in the open.</p>
  </aside>

  <article>
    <section class="section article-section">
      <div class="shell article-grid">
        <div class="article-marker mono">WHY V3</div>
        <div class="prose article-prose">
          <h2>The activity stream still works. The query language needs to catch up.</h2>
          <p>Activity Schema 2.0 made an important separation: model independent business activities once, then relate them when a question is asked. The stream remains the stable center of the model—an entity, an activity, a timestamp, an identifier, and useful features.</p>
          <p>But the original relationship names combine several decisions. “Last before” means both <em>fetch the last match</em> and <em>look before this cohort row</em>. That is easy to say, but harder for software to validate, extend, and compile consistently.</p>
          <p>Polyform’s Dataset tool now represents those decisions independently. It also supports same-object joins, reusable dimensions, time-valid dimensional history, filters, refinements, and explicit row-expanding relationships. V3 proposes bringing that production grammar back into the open standard.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section article-section white-section">
      <div class="shell article-grid">
        <div class="article-marker mono">THE GRAMMAR</div>
        <div class="article-prose">
          <h2>Split every temporal relationship into fetch + relation.</h2>
          <p class="lede-small">The familiar names remain useful for people. The two-axis form is more useful for systems.</p>
          <div class="mapping-table" role="table" aria-label="V2 relationship names mapped to V3 fetch and relation">
            <div class="mapping-head" role="row">
              <span role="columnheader">Readable name</span><span role="columnheader">Fetch</span><span role="columnheader">Relation</span><span role="columnheader">Meaning</span>
            </div>
            {relationshipMappings.map(([name, fetch, relation, meaning]) => (
              <div class="mapping-row" role="row">
                <strong role="cell">{name}</strong><code role="cell">{fetch}</code><code role="cell">{relation}</code><span role="cell">{meaning}</span>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
          <div class="axis-grid">
            <div class="axis-card">
              <span class="axis-label mono">FETCH</span>
              <div><code>first</code><code>last</code><code>all</code><code>metric</code></div>
              <p>Which matching occurrence—or aggregate—should become output?</p>
            </div>
            <div class="axis-card">
              <span class="axis-label mono">RELATION</span>
              <div><code>ever</code><code>before</code><code>before_or_on</code><code>after</code><code>after_or_on</code><code>in_between</code></div>
              <p>Where in time should the compiler look relative to the cohort row?</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section article-section">
      <div class="shell article-grid">
        <div class="article-marker mono">THE CHANGES</div>
        <div class="change-list">
          {changes.map((change) => (
            <section class="change-row">
              <span class="change-number mono">{change.number}</span>
              <div><h3>{change.title}</h3><p>{change.body}</p></div>
            </section>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section article-section dark-section">
      <div class="shell article-grid">
        <div class="article-marker mono">ONE PLAN</div>
        <div class="article-prose dark-prose">
          <h2>Time decides when. Joins decide what belongs together.</h2>
          <p>Consider an order dataset. The cohort might be every <code>completed_order</code>. The next shipment is a <code>shipped_order</code> fetched <code>first</code> <code>after</code> the order. A same-object join on <code>order_id</code> prevents the compiler from attaching a different order’s shipment simply because it occurred next.</p>
          <div class="relationship-plan">
            <div><span class="mono">COHORT</span><code>all completed_order</code></div>
            <i aria-hidden="true">+</i>
            <div><span class="mono">FETCH · RELATION</span><code>first · after · shipped_order</code></div>
            <i aria-hidden="true">+</i>
            <div><span class="mono">JOIN</span><code>cohort.order_id = append.order_id</code></div>
          </div>
          <p>A product dimension can then attach category or hierarchy. If that reference table records historical versions, an effective timestamp lets the compiler select the latest version before the activity occurred. Temporal relationships and slowly changing dimensions are related ideas, but they answer different questions and deserve different parts of the contract.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section article-section white-section">
      <div class="shell article-grid">
        <div class="article-marker mono">EXAMPLE</div>
        <div class="article-prose">
          <h2>A V3 query is data before it is SQL.</h2>
          <p>The first public compiler uses a deliberately small JSON form. The important part is that the plan is declarative and contains no executable SQL fragments.</p>
          <pre class="code-block"><code>{`{
  "version": "3",
  "dialect": "snowflake",
  "stream": {
    "entity": "customer",
    "schema": "analytics",
    "table": "activity_stream",
    "layout": "single_table"
  },
  "cohort": {
    "activity": "completed_order",
    "fetch": "all",
    "filters": [{
      "column": "country",
      "type": "string",
      "operator": "equal",
      "value": "US"
    }],
    "columns": [
      { "name": "ts", "alias": "ordered_at", "type": "timestamp" },
      { "name": "order_id", "alias": "order_id", "type": "string" },
      { "name": "segment", "alias": "account_segment", "type": "string", "dimension": "account" }
    ],
    "dimensions": [{
      "key": "account",
      "schema": "analytics",
      "table": "account_history",
      "joins": [{
        "activityColumn": "account_id",
        "dimensionColumn": "account_id",
        "type": "string"
      }],
      "slowlyChangingTimestamp": "valid_from"
    }]
  },
  "appends": [{
    "key": "shipment",
    "activity": "shipped_order",
    "fetch": "first",
    "relation": "after",
    "filters": [{
      "column": "is_test",
      "type": "boolean",
      "operator": "equal",
      "value": false
    }],
    "columns": [
      { "name": "ts", "alias": "shipped_at", "type": "timestamp" },
      { "name": "carrier", "alias": "carrier", "type": "string" }
    ],
    "joins": [{
      "cohortColumn": "order_id",
      "appendColumn": "order_id",
      "type": "string"
    }]
  }]
}`}</code></pre>
          <p>The compiler can read the canonical single-stream layout or the optional table-per-activity layout, explain the row grain, validate the combination, quote identifiers for the warehouse, and generate the repetitive temporal SQL. It does not need warehouse credentials, and it does not need to run the query.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section article-section ecosystem-section">
      <div class="shell article-grid">
        <div class="article-marker mono">ECOSYSTEM</div>
        <div class="article-prose">
          <h2>This should be an open query grammar, not a product-only feature.</h2>
          <p>The V3 proposal is informed by the Dataset compiler Polyform operates in production, but it is intended to remain portable. Existing community work already points in the same direction. The <a href="https://github.com/bcodell/dbt-activity-schema">dbt Activity Schema proof of concept</a> provides a macro interface and an experimental Activity Query Language for creating denormalized datasets from an activity stream.</p>
          <p>The next contribution from Polyform is an unauthenticated compiler and MCP server. Any compatible AI client will be able to submit a V3 query plan and receive warehouse SQL, an explanation, output columns, and warnings—without connecting the service to private data.</p>
          <div class="actions">
            <a class="button button-primary" href="../mcp/">Try Activity Schema MCP <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
            <a class="button button-secondary" href="https://github.com/ActivitySchema/ActivitySchema/blob/main/2.0.md">Read canonical V2</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .article-hero { max-width: 1240px; }
  .article-hero h1 { max-width: 1120px; }
  .status-note { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 110px 1fr; gap: 28px; padding: 22px; border: 1px solid var(--peach); background: rgba(242, 147, 107, 0.08); }
  .status-note > span { color: var(--orange); font-size: 0.67rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em; }
  .status-note p { max-width: 920px; margin: 0; font-size: 0.92rem; }
  .article-section { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .article-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 150px minmax(0, 860px); justify-content: space-between; gap: clamp(36px, 8vw, 120px); }
  .article-marker { padding-top: 8px; color: var(--seafoam-dark); font-size: 0.66rem; letter-spacing: 0.12em; }
  .article-prose h2 { max-width: 850px; }
  .article-prose p { font-size: 1.08rem; line-height: 1.75; }
  .article-prose a { color: var(--seafoam-dark); }
  .white-section { background: var(--white); }
  .lede-small { max-width: 680px; color: var(--steel); font-size: 1.1rem; }
  .mapping-table { margin-top: 48px; border-top: 1px solid var(--navy); }
  .mapping-head, .mapping-row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.05fr .65fr .9fr 1.8fr; gap: 16px; padding: 14px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); align-items: baseline; }
  .mapping-head { color: var(--steel); font-size: 0.67rem; letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .mapping-row { font-size: .86rem; }
  .mapping-row strong { color: var(--navy); }
  .mapping-row span { color: var(--steel); }
  .axis-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 20px; margin-top: 48px; }
  .axis-card { padding: 24px; border: 1px solid var(--line); background: var(--canvas); }
  .axis-label { color: var(--orange); font-size: .65rem; }
  .axis-card > div { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 7px; margin: 30px 0 22px; }
  .axis-card code { padding: 5px 8px; background: var(--white); font-size: .7rem; }
  .axis-card p { margin: 0; font-size: .86rem; }
  .change-list { border-top: 1px solid var(--navy); }
  .change-row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 54px 1fr; gap: 26px; padding: 32px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .change-number { color: var(--orange); font-size: .67rem; }
  .change-row h3 { font-size: 1.4rem; }
  .change-row p { max-width: 700px; margin: 0; line-height: 1.7; }
  .dark-section { background: var(--midnight); }
  .dark-section .article-marker { color: var(--seafoam); }
  .dark-prose h2 { color: var(--white); }
  .dark-prose p { color: rgba(255,255,255,.68); }
  .dark-prose code { color: var(--seafoam-light); }
  .relationship-plan { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1.3fr auto 1.45fr; gap: 14px; align-items: center; margin: 46px 0; }
  .relationship-plan > div { display: grid; min-height: 118px; align-content: space-between; gap: 30px; padding: 16px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.16); }
  .relationship-plan span { color: rgba(255,255,255,.45); font-size: .58rem; }
  .relationship-plan i { color: var(--seafoam); font-style: normal; }
  .code-block { overflow-x: auto; margin: 40px 0 30px; padding: 26px; background: var(--midnight); color: var(--white); font-size: .82rem; line-height: 1.65; }
  .code-block code { color: var(--seafoam-light); }
  .ecosystem-section { background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--canvas), rgba(68, 180, 139, .08)); }
  @media (max-width: 820px) {
    .status-note, .article-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .article-marker { padding: 0; }
    .mapping-head { display: none; }
    .mapping-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
    .mapping-row span { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
    .axis-grid, .relationship-plan { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .relationship-plan i { justify-self: center; }
  }
</style>
